# 17. Behind the Scenes (Internal Working)

When Python executes:

```python
for i in range(1, n + 1):
```

it first creates a `range` object. A `range` object does **not** store every number in memory. Instead, it stores only the start, stop, and step values and computes each number as needed during iteration. This makes `range()` very memory-efficient.

Each iteration assigns the next generated value to `i`, executes the loop body, and then requests the next value until the sequence is exhausted.

Similarly, `print(i, end="")` internally writes the value to the standard output stream. By default, `print()` appends a newline character (`\n`), but `end=""` replaces it with an empty string, causing all numbers to appear consecutively on the same line. This behind-the-scenes behavior explains why the solution uses constant extra memory while still producing the required output.
